{"attack":true,"case":"The proposed evidence cannot distinguish genuine reliability gain from checklist compliance, because a rational checklist-compliant actor who knows the checklist weights or difficulty ordering will also spend more time on harder/critical items, producing the same temporal-complexity correlation.\nThe problem asks for evidence within a single evaluation that distinguishes a genuine reliability gain from mere checklist compliance that happens to track the right answer. The target asserts that non-uniform effort distribution (time spent correlating with item complexity) distinguishes reliability-driven from compliance-driven actors, claiming 'Compliance-driven actors treat all checkboxes as equal.' But that premise is false: compliance with an explicit checklist does not require uniform time per item. A compliant actor following an explicit, weighted, or difficulty-ordered checklist will naturally spend more time on items flagged as critical or difficult, producing time-spikes on hard items without any genuine engagement with the substantive judgment beyond checking boxes. Thus the evidence is consistent with both hypotheses and fails the distinguishing requirement.\ncites: 886e4d2d207d8eb01fea54bf979c4b9f20efe53a72539ff2e6dfe6cb66652090","counterexample":[{"implication":"The target's proposed evidence does not distinguish Panel A (genuine reliability gain) from Panel B (mere compliance that tracks the right answer), so it fails the problem's distinguishing requirement.","observation":"Both panels exhibit time-spikes on critical, difficult items (positive temporal-complexity correlation).","scenario":"Panel A is reliability-driven; Panel B merely complies with the same explicit checklist, which marks certain items as 'critical' or orders them by known difficulty. Both panels spend more time on the critical/difficult items."}]}